- Story & Context: When audio archivist Jonah Vance baked the corroded 1974 time-capsule tape in an attic toaster oven to stop the ferric oxide shedding, the melted ribbon fused to his tape player's capstan, releasing an acrid plastic stench and accidentally doubling the tape's speed. Desperate to salvage the recording, he ran the shrieking playback through a pair of high-gain surf amps just as an unseasonal swarm of migrating beach locusts pelted the metal siding of his shed in rhythmic clatter. The playback exploded into a blinding sprint of staccato double-picking that mirrored the insects' frantic wings, immortalizing Jonah’s panicked broadcast over the tracking mic: "Cut the throttle, let the salt burn down the antenna!"
